    {
        "content": "<p>Higher order functions are not supported. It is definitely something I would like to do at some point, mainly to automate calculations in variational calculus, but unfortunately I don't know when I find the time to do that if ever <span aria-label=\"cry\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f622\" role=\"img\" title=\"cry\">:cry:</span></p>\n<p>The normal first order fun_prop needs couple of lambda calculus rules, id, const, comp, apply, pi, prodMk, fat, snd. With those you can decompose first order non-dependet function into into its atoms.</p>\n<p>In variational calculus, some if these basic rules fail. If I remember correctly, apply rule does not work because it's variational gradient would have to be Dirac delta. For higher order functions you can come up with more refined set of lambda calculus rules, but I still do not know what is the \"minimal\" set of rules.</p>\n<p>Anyway, I'm curious, for your predicate <code>MonoPred</code> can you state and prove the full set of rules, id, const, comp, apply, pi, prodMk, fat, snd?</p>\n<p>Also you can probably sidestep the issue by defining <code>MonoPred</code> to be on functions between sets, that way you hide the \"higher order\" nature of the function.</p>",
        "id": 611236094,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1784276592
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just for a reference here is a test file stating all the lambda calculus rules </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/fd1d54bcac5caba4eff2ea3421c47d907333f515/MathlibTest/FunPropMinimal.lean#L40\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/fd1d54bcac5caba4eff2ea3421c47d907333f515/MathlibTest/FunPropMinimal.lean#L40</a></p>",
        "id": 611236548,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1784276742
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Product rules are a bit lower</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/fd1d54bcac5caba4eff2ea3421c47d907333f515/MathlibTest/FunPropMinimal.lean#L72\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/fd1d54bcac5caba4eff2ea3421c47d907333f515/MathlibTest/FunPropMinimal.lean#L72</a></p>",
        "id": 611236577,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1784276754
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks, using <code>Set</code> as a wrapper makes it work. Interestingly enough it's not required for the const rule:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">fun_prop</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- no complaint</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">monotone_const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoPred</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">λ_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">χ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mono_pred</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ψ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hχ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hχ</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 611249850,
        "sender_full_name": "Oliver Soeser",
        "timestamp": 1784281473
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah, the <code>fun_prop</code> attribute does some kind of analysis of the lambda term and I guess it has a bit strange/unexpected behavior in edge cases.</p>",
